WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:06.620
Hi.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:09.700
I'm Garrick Aden-Buie, a Data
Science Educator at RStudio

00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.530
and an R and Shiny developer.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.270
I also tinker with
web development,

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:16.149
and I realized that I've learned
the most about web development

00:00:16.149 --> 00:00:18.190
by working on my slides.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:20.050
Wanting to put images
in the right place

00:00:20.050 --> 00:00:21.790
or give text the
right color, it's

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.520
like each slide ends up
becoming a small web project.

00:00:24.520 --> 00:00:26.860
And I've learned more about
web development this way

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:27.910
than any other.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:30.050
So that's what I want
to share with you today.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.110
How can we use xaringan
as a playground

00:00:32.110 --> 00:00:34.430
to learn web development?

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:35.330
So wait, what?

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:36.880
What is xaringan?

00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.600
First of all, it's in R package
that you can install from CRAN.

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.850
Second, it's a way to
write in R Markdown

00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:46.000
with some extra syntax, and
then have your R Markdown, R

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.120
code, and outputs, plots,
widgets all rendered

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.260
into slides.

00:00:50.260 --> 00:00:53.055
The slides are
web pages in HTML.

00:00:53.055 --> 00:00:54.430
And the great
thing about that is

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.550
that you can CSS to layout
and style your slides.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.260
But the downside
is that you have

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:02.290
to use CSS to layout
and style your slides.

00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:05.000
So CSS is a little tricky
to wrap your mind around,

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:07.870
but I think that xaringan
makes it a little more fun.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:12.130
So let's first learn a little
bit about CSS and the web.

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:15.100
CSS styles are rules about
how elements on the screen

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:16.190
should appear.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:18.730
The most common way to
link a rule to an element

00:01:18.730 --> 00:01:20.110
is to use a class.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:22.620
In CSS, these start with a dot.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.520
Xaringan has an
extra little syntax

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.550
that makes it easier to
reference the CSS classes.

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:30.520
You write the class name,
then a pair of brackets,

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.930
and then you put
text or whatever

00:01:31.930 --> 00:01:33.850
you want inside the brackets.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.820
Here we use the complement
class for the whole sentence

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:41.110
and the rstudio-blue class
for just the word RStudio.

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.540
Inside a rule, there
are CSS properties

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.840
where you can set a
property equal to a value

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:51.790
like font family to
monospace or color to tomato.

00:01:51.790 --> 00:01:54.880
Notice that because rstudio-blue
has its own color,

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.740
and it's closer to
the word RStudio,

00:01:56.740 --> 00:01:58.210
it wraps around RStudio.

00:01:58.210 --> 00:02:01.270
It doesn't turn tomato.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:02.860
The second thing to
know about the web

00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:06.070
is that everything is in
a box. The block boxes

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:08.830
take a full line of
horizontal space.

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:12.640
In Markdown, their brackets
spend more than one line.

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:15.100
Inline boxes fit
within block boxes,

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.660
and don't break the flow.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.180
In Markdown, their brackets
are on the same line

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:20.980
like with .rstudio-blue.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:24.940
The real reason xaringan is so
fun is infinite moon reader,

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:25.900
let me show you why.

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:31.180
OK, I have a little project
here with some slides.

00:02:31.180 --> 00:02:32.890
It's an RMD file,
and I've cleared

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:34.550
out all of the starter text.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:38.430
So let's add a heading,
and render the slides.

00:02:38.430 --> 00:02:39.290
Great!

00:02:39.290 --> 00:02:41.690
But now let's use
infinite moon reader.

00:02:41.690 --> 00:02:44.210
It's available under
the Addins menu.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.480
When we run it, it first
renders the slides.

00:02:47.480 --> 00:02:51.260
But when I type, my text
shows up right away.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:53.040
This is awesome.

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:56.210
So let's recreate the
example we saw in the slides.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:59.660
I have a compliment
that RStudio is cool,

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:02.670
and I'll put RStudio
in rstudio-blue.

00:03:02.670 --> 00:03:05.150
OK, that takes care
of the Markdown.

00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:07.760
But we can add HTML to the
slides too, which means

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.080
I can create a style block.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:10.820
And create rules
for the compliment

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:12.020
in rstudio-blue color.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:20.750
Look, the slides
are updated live.

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:22.900
Are we having fun?

00:03:22.900 --> 00:03:27.610
Classes are reusable, so
let's make fun blue too.

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.630
OK, one more example.

00:03:29.630 --> 00:03:32.770
Let's create a black
element called cat box.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.200
In the box, we'll put a cat.

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.740
It's hard to see
the black element,

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:39.440
so let's give it a
background color.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.380
Now you can see it
takes up the whole line,

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:47.630
and let's give it a height and
a width say 300 pixels each.

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:49.620
Rather than have it be
a blue background, let's

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.470
actually put a cat in the box.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:55.950
For this, we can use
background-image with url(),

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:00.240
and then a URL to a
placeholder kitten image.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.240
Now, usually cats on
slides are positioned off

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.610
to the side a bit, and
we can do this too.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:08.190
We'll set position
to absolute, which

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:11.730
tells the browser to put the box
exactly where we want it to be

00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:15.490
relative to the top, right,
bottom, or left of the box's

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.800
container, which is our slide.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:21.930
So let's put it about 50 pixels
from the bottom and 50 pixels

00:04:21.930 --> 00:04:23.550
from the right.

00:04:23.550 --> 00:04:24.630
Great!

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:27.660
One last note. When you're done,
you should put these styles

00:04:27.660 --> 00:04:28.860
in a CSS file.

00:04:35.730 --> 00:04:38.640
OK, I hope you enjoyed
this and learned a little.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.140
Go explore and have fun.

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:41.970
And also, check
out xaringanExtra,

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:44.790
it's an R package that
brings lots of little goodies

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:45.960
to xaringan.

00:04:45.960 --> 00:04:48.170
Go make great slides.

